WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.511
Welcome to Module 2 Designing Relational Databases

00:00:04.447 --> 00:00:06.184
In this module we are going to

00:00:06.211 --> 00:00:08.408
focus on the structure that makes reliable

00:00:08.434 --> 00:00:12.847
SQL and analytics possible later Before we

00:00:12.873 --> 00:00:14.991
worry about writing long queries we need

00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:17.386
to decide what each table represents what

00:00:17.412 --> 00:00:19.741
one row means how tables connect and

00:00:19.768 --> 00:00:22.360
what rules the database should protect automatically

00:00:24.111 --> 00:00:26.479
The examples use a small outpatient clinic

00:00:26.703 --> 00:00:28.532
but the design ideas apply to many

00:00:28.558 --> 00:00:31.584
domains such as retail education finance and

00:00:31.611 --> 00:00:36.422
operations You will also see small PostgreSQL

00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:38.764
examples that run directly in the browser

00:00:39.688 --> 00:00:41.359
When a code slide appears run it

00:00:41.385 --> 00:00:43.556
here first inspect the result and if

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.543
you want more practice use Copy and

00:00:45.557 --> 00:00:47.793
paste the same SQL into the pgAdmin

00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:51.345
Query Tool The goal is not to

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:54.532
memorize a complete schema The goal is

00:00:54.545 --> 00:00:56.901
to understand the design decisions behind the

00:00:56.927 --> 00:00:57.427
schema
